{
 "what": "GitLab-native scope comments (eval/gitlab_scope_rubric.json, registered before any note was fetched, amended twice before any note was read): on merge requests GitLab Duo's Developer Flow opened in gitlab-org/gitlab (created 2026-06-01 to 2026-10-18, merged or closed), the notes people other than the author wrote that say part of the change did not belong (two blind model labellers, Haiku and Sonnet, splits settled by an Opus adjudicator), and whether rules v6 listed the file each one points at, on the diff version the person saw.",
 "reading": "In plain words: people at GitLab objected to part of an agent's change in 77 of 1537 labelled notes; 65 of those point at a file we could score; the rules listed none of the 65. 43 of the 65 are Ruby source or spec files and 10 are docs: kinds the rules are not built to list (they list dependencies, CI and config files, agent instructions, generated and binary files). Only 1 of the 65 is on such a file (a CI rules file whose line the asked-for removal had made dead), and it was not listed either. On these merge requests, the rules do not catch what GitLab's reviewers object to.",
 "estimate_whole_frame": "about 196 scope comments among the 4031 people's notes (scaled from the random draw only: 73 of 1500); an estimate",
 "limits": "One project, one agent, four and a half months. Labels are model readings of people's notes, not a person's; on the fixed audit sample the adjudicator agreed with both labellers on 207 of 208. A reviewer's words are not proof the file did not belong, and reviewers stay silent about many extras. The rules were not tuned on these notes.",
